e('You can measure this on Linux with `perf stat -e dTLB-load-misses ./prog`.','On supported Linux PMUs, `perf stat -e dTLB-load-misses ./prog` can help investigate. Check `perf list`, event semantics, permissions and CPU model: a generic event need not count every L1-TLB miss.','PMUeventsnotidenticaleveryCPU.')
e('A program whose hot data is larger than the reach will miss constantly.','A hot set beyond the effective reach can increase misses; locality, associativity, page-size-specific capacities and other translation-cache levels determine the rate.','Reachnotalonepredictconstmiss.')
e('A database with a 50 GiB buffer pool has a working set thousands of times bigger than a 6 MiB reach.','If all 50 GiB of a buffer pool is actively accessed, it greatly exceeds a hypothetical 6 MiB reach. Allocated pool capacity need not equal the hot working set.','Allocatedmemorynotworkingset.')
e('Each TLB entry is tagged with the address space it belongs to, so entries from several processes can coexist and no flush is needed.','Tags let translations from several address spaces coexist, reducing flushes. Mapping changes and tag reuse still require invalidation.','ASIDdoesnoteliminateflush.',fl)
e('PCIDs became essential after **KPTI** (lesson 1): each process now has separate user and kernel page tables, so every system call switches CR3 twice.','PCIDs help reduce **KPTI** overhead (lesson 1). When KPTI is active, a userspace system call switches between reduced user and full kernel page-table views. KPTI also works without PCIDs.','Notessential/allCPUsKPTIon.',pt)
e('Each shootdown costs microseconds and interrupts every core involved.','Shootdown cost varies with hardware, target CPUs and kernel policy. The IPI sequence above is a common implementation, not a requirement that every unmap interrupt every core; deferred invalidation and architectural broadcast mechanisms may differ.','No universalmeasurement/IPImethod.',fl)
