e('**Transparent huge pages (THP)** are controlled by `/sys/kernel/mm/transparent_hugepage/enabled`:','For anonymous THP, `/sys/kernel/mm/transparent_hugepage/enabled` is a top-level policy inherited by configured sizes. Modern kernels also have per-size, process and mapping controls. In the simple inherited-policy case:','Notsolecontrol.',th)
e('- `always`: the kernel tries to use huge pages for any large enough anonymous mapping.','- `always`: eligible allocations may use THP, subject to size, mapping, process and allocation constraints.','Notanyregion.',th)
e('- `madvise`: only for regions where the program called `madvise(addr, len, MADV_HUGEPAGE)`.','- `madvise`: ordinary automatic allocation is limited to advised eligible regions.','MADVCOLLAPSEexception.',th)
e('- `never`: off.','- `never`: disables this automatic policy, but is not an absolute prohibition: `MADV_COLLAPSE` can bypass the sysfs setting, and per-size controls may differ. Use the documented process/mapping controls when full exclusion is required.','ImportantmodernTHPneverexception.',th)
e('which can stall an allocation for milliseconds.','which can stall an allocation by a workload-dependent amount.','Unsupportedexacttiming.',th)
e('gets 2 MiB of RAM per region instead of 4 KiB.','may allocate a 2 MiB page per region instead of a base page, depending on policy and fault type.','Notdeterministicallocsize.',th)
e('After `fork`, writing one byte to a shared huge page copies 2 MiB instead of 4 KiB.','After `fork`, a write fault on a shared huge page can copy a larger unit, split a mapping, or reuse exclusive backing depending on kernel state and policy; it does not invariably copy 2 MiB.','Hugewritefaultcan split/reuse.',th+['https://raw.githubusercontent.com/torvalds/linux/master/mm/huge_memory.c'])
e('So THP is usually best set to `madvise`, and databases that benefit (PostgreSQL with `huge_pages = try`, the JVM with large-page options, Oracle, VMs) opt in explicitly, often with hugetlbfs so the memory is reserved and never fragmented.','Choose THP or explicit HugeTLB based on measured workload and application guidance. PostgreSQL `huge_pages` controls explicit huge pages for its main shared-memory region, not a request for THP. Explicit reservations may fail and require sizing; reserved contiguous pages do not imply fragmentation is impossible elsewhere.','Unjustifiedglobalrecommendation andexplicitvsTHP.',th+['https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/runtime-config-resource.html'])
e('- The TLB caches VPN-to-PFN translations with permissions; a hit costs nearly nothing, a miss costs a page walk of up to four or five memory reads.','- TLBs cache translations and permissions. A miss can hit another translation-cache level or require a walk; cost depends on the CPU and mapping.','Summarymatcheshierarchy.')
e('- Unmapping memory in a multithreaded process needs a TLB shootdown via IPIs.','- Mapping removal requires coherent invalidation of stale translations; remote shootdowns commonly use IPIs.','Notsolemechanism.',fl)
e('- Huge pages help large working sets but can add compaction latency, memory bloat and copy-on-write cost; prefer `madvise` or explicit hugetlbfs.','- Huge pages can improve translation reach while adding compaction, memory and COW costs. Follow application-specific guidance and measure the workload.','No universalchoice.',th)
